.TH RC 1
.SH NAME
rc \- command language (in progress - foundation only, no shell yet)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B rc
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Rc
is a real translation of Plan 9's
.IR rc (1),
from
.B utils/rcsh/
in this tree - not a workalike. It is being built incrementally; see
.B doc/plan9-terminal-plan.md
for why (a real
.I rc
is what
.IR mk (1)
needs to run genuine Plan 9 mkfile recipes) and for the finding that
changed this translation's shape before it started:
.B utils/rcsh
was never a portable
.IR rc .
Its own mkfile refuses to build for anything but Windows NT/95 -
.B Nt.c
is the only platform binding ever written, and no Posix binding ever
existed here. So this translation is really two different kinds of
work: a real, portable
.I rc
language core (lexer, grammar, word lists, variables, the code-vector
execution engine) to translate directly from
.BR utils/rcsh 's
own source, and an entirely new platform layer (process spawn/wait,
pipes) to design against Limbo's
.B spawn
and channel model from scratch, since there is no Posix reference to
translate that part from.
.PP
This version implements seven slices - a real grammar, a real
tree-to-source printer, and a real compiler to an instruction vector
now, but still nothing that RUNS one - see BUGS. The first is word
lists and the variable/keyword tables from
.B word.c
and
.BR var.c .
The second is
.BR glob.c 's
pure pattern-matching logic
.RI ( deglob ,
.IR match ,
.IR matchfn ,
and a reduced
.I haswild
standing in for
.BR globsize 's
buffer-sizing side effect, which Limbo strings don't need) plus
.BR word.c 's
.IR subwords ,
deferred from the first slice because it needs
.BR deglob .
.BR glob.c 's
directory-walking half
.RI ( globdir ,
.IR glob ,
.IR globsort ,
.IR globlist )
stays deferred - it needs a real argument stack and real directory
reads, the same boundary the first slice's deferred
.IR pushword / popword / pushlist / poplist
already are.
.PP
The third is
.BR tree.c 's
parse-tree node constructors
.RI ( tree1 ,
.IR tree2 ,
.IR tree3 ,
.IR mung1 ,
.IR mung2 ,
.IR mung3 ,
.IR epimung ,
.IR token ),
plus the real
.B klook
from
.BR var.c ,
now that
.B Tree
exists for it to build. These are called
.B by
grammar actions, not coupled to the lexer the way the lexer and
grammar are coupled to each other, so they're testable the same way
the first two slices were.
.B simplemung
is deferred - it needs a tree-to-source pretty-printer
.RI ( pfmt (s,
.RB \(dq %t \(dq,
.IR t ),
from
.B io.c
/
.BR pfnc.c )
that doesn't exist yet.
.PP
The fourth is
.BR lex.c ,
the tokenizer - the first slice large enough, and coupled enough
internally, that it isn't split further, and the first to abandon
.IR rc 's
own input source
.RI ( runq->cmdfd ,
the current thread's input) for an in-memory string, since there is
no Thread yet and real lexical scanning doesn't care where its
characters come from. Real
.IR rc 's
own interactive bookkeeping in
.B getnext()
.RI ( doprompt / pprompt()
and a
.B flag['V']-gated
echo) is dropped, not translated, for the same reason - it's tied to
a real Thread this version doesn't have yet. Word/glob/quote/
redirection-arrow scanning, keyword lookup via
.BR klook ,
and the
.B ^-insertion
/
.B SUB-detection
adjacency logic for
.RI ( $ var (n))
subscripting are all translated and tested directly against a string
input, with no lexer/grammar coupling problem in practice: this
version supplies its own token constants (see the
.B Rc
module's own declaration), so - unlike real
.IR rc ,
where yacc generates those constants FROM the grammar - nothing here
actually needs the grammar to exist first.
.PP
The fifth is
.BR syn.y ,
the grammar - hand-written recursive descent, following
.IR hoc (1)'s
own precedent for a yacc-based real source (a module-global
.B curtok
plus a parameterless
.BR padvance() ,
matching
.IR hoc (1)'s
own
.B curtok
/
.BR advance() ,
not the explicit-Lexstate-parameter style this file's own lexer
section uses one layer down - the grammar is new machinery replacing
yacc, not more of the lexer). Real
.IR rc 's
.B %left
/
.B %right
priority table is
.B not
a precedence-climbing chain the way
.IR hoc (1)'s
arithmetic operators are - most of it resolves yacc shift/reduce
conflicts in an ambiguous grammar rather than encoding a clean
operator hierarchy. Two lines are real precedence and became the
loop-based chain here:
.B %left ANDAND OROR
(loosest), then
.B %left PIPE
(tighter). The
.B %left BANG SUBSHELL
line and the two
.B %prec BANG
annotations on
.B redir cmd
and
.B assign cmd
put all four prefix forms
.RI ( ! ,
.IR @ ,
a leading redirection, a leading assignment) at one shared level
strictly between those two, confirmed by hand against two concrete
cases before any of this was written, not assumed: BANG's own rule
precedence is
.B lower
than ANDAND's, so
.B "! a && b"
is
.B "(!a) && b"
- the operand of any of those four prefixes is parsed by the
PIPE-looping level, never the looser ANDAND/OROR-looping top level.
A prefix redirection's rule precedence is
.B lower
than PIPE's, so
.B ">f a | b"
is
.B ">f (a|b)"
- that same operand
.B does
extend through PIPE. IF/WHILE/FOR/SWITCH sit at the
.B opposite
extreme on purpose: their own rule precedence is the lowest in the
whole table, lower than ANDAND/OROR too, so their body always
shift-extends through everything -
.B "if(x) a && b"
is
.B "if(x) (a&&b)" .
See BUGS for two real findings this precedence work turned up.
.PP
.BR word 's
own
.B "keyword {$1->type=WORD;}"
alternative - a bare keyword can start a command
.RI ( if ,
.IR while ", ...)"
but can never be the command's own name, so
.B "echo if"
must treat
.I if
as a plain argument word - is handled by demoting a keyword token's
own
.B ttype
to
.B Tword
wherever a
.I word
(not
.IR first )
is expected; reaching
.I first
itself with a keyword token is structurally unreachable, not
separately guarded, since every keyword already has its own case in
the dispatch that would otherwise fall through to
.IR first .
.PP
One grammar action is only partially real, noted where it's defined:
.B heredoc()
returns a clearly-marked placeholder WORD token in the real tag's
own place - the real one opens a temp file that
.B exec.c
later fills with the heredoc body (source lines read up to a line
matching the tag), needing a source-reading front end this
translation doesn't have (no Thread, no interactive/file reader). It
lets
.B <<TAG
parse into the right shape now (see the sixth slice's own BUGS entry
below for what its printed form actually looks like); it cannot run
anything.
.B simplemung()
itself is now fully real, including its
.B str
field, since
.BR pcmd() " (the sixth slice, below) gave it a real"
.B "pfmt(s, \(dq%t\(dq, t)"
to call.
.PP
.BR treeshape() ,
exposed only to
.IR rctest (1),
is a test-only structural dump
.RI ( "(TYPENAME child...)" )
- a different tool for a different job than real
.IR rc 's
own
.B %t
pretty-printer (which reproduces
.I rc
source text): it exists purely so a parser test can compare a whole
tree in one assertion instead of walking
.BR .child / .ttype / .str
by hand the way earlier slices had to.
.PP
The sixth is
.BR pcmd.c ,
real
.IR rc 's
own
.B %t
tree-to-source pretty-printer, brought forward ahead of
.B code.c
on purpose: two things were blocked on it before either could be
real -
.BR code.c 's
own
.B fnstr()
(the source text
.BR Xasync / Xbackq / Xsubshell / Xpipe
hand
.B exec.c
as what to actually run) and
.BR simplemung() 's
own
.B str
field, deferred since the fourth slice. Both are done now:
.B simplemung()
sets a real
.B str
(see BUGS for what it actually contains), and
.B fnstr()
exists here as
.IR pcmd() " with the statement separator forced to"
.B ;
instead of a real newline - real
.IR rc 's
own
.B "\(dqchange to semicolon for bourne-proofing\(dq"
comment on its global
.B nl
variable, which
.B nlchar
stands in for here the same way
.B curlocal
/
.B iflast
already stand in for other pieces of real
.IR rc 's
per-thread state this version doesn't have yet. Pure tree-to-string
recursion with no
.B runq
/Thread dependency at all, same shape as
.BR tree.c 's
own node constructors - real
.B pcmd()
writes into an
.B Io
buffer via
.BR pfmt ; this version needs none of that intermediate buffered-writer
machinery at all, since Limbo string concatenation already builds the
whole result directly, and returns the string rather than writing
anywhere. Real
.BR pcmd() 's
own
.B pdeglob()
helper - print a word with each GLOB escape byte stripped, printing
whatever follows it - is not a new function here: it is glob.c's own
.BR deglob() ,
byte for byte, reused directly rather than reimplemented. Tested by
round-tripping this parser's own real output
.RI ( testparseprint() " = " parsecmd() " then " pcmd() ),
the same reuse the parser's own tests already got from the lexer,
rather than hand-building a tree for every case. See BUGS for two
real findings this turned up, both in the same upstream case.
.PP
The seventh is
.BR code.c ,
tree-to-instruction-vector compilation
.RI ( outcode() / codeswitch() / iscase() / compile() ) -
still a pure tree walk, still nothing that RUNS the vector it
produces (that's
.BR exec.c ,
not translated yet). Stops exactly at
.BR compile() ,
the same boundary real
.B code.c
itself draws:
.BR codecopy() / codefree()
are reference-counting and bulk-free bookkeeping for the compiled
vector, the same "no counterpart under a real garbage collector"
category as
.BR tree.c 's
own
.BR Treenodes / freenodes ,
not translated for the same reason.
.B cleanhere()
is deferred along with
.BR heredoc() .
Real
.B compile() 's
own
.B readhere()
call is already commented out in the real source itself - that's
upstream's own state, not something dropped here.
.PP
Real
.B Code
is a C union with no tag of its own; the real
.BR emitf / emiti / emits
macros just write whichever field a given instruction's own fixed
layout calls for, and the reader (real
.BR exec.c )
knows which fields to expect at which offset from the opcode alone.
.B Codecell
here keeps all three fields on every cell instead of a union (Limbo
has no untagged union) - costs a little memory, changes nothing about
how any cell is used.
.BR stuffdot() " (patching a forward jump target into an ALREADY-"
EMITTED cell) needs random-access mutation of one earlier slot, so
.B codebuf
is a real growable array plus a fill pointer, grown in chunks the way
real
.B morecode()
grows its own
.BR realloc ,
not a list.
.BR emitop() / emitint() / emitstr()
all return the index they wrote, matching the real macros' own
C pre-increment idiom
.RI ( "p=emiti(0)"
captures the index just written, for a later
.B stuffdot(p)
to patch) - an off-by-one here would silently corrupt every jump
target while still looking like a plausible address in a
.B codeshape()
dump, which is exactly the kind of bug this slice's own tests (below)
are built to catch.
.B runq->iflast
(real
.B outcode()
reads and writes it, and
.B "case NOT"
raises a real error if it isn't set) is the same kind of real
per-thread parse-time state
.B curlocal
/
.B nlchar
already stand in for - a module-global
.B iflast
does the same job, correct for the same reason: there is only one
thing compiling at a time right now.
.PP
Tested by compiling this parser's own real output
.RI ( testcompile() " = " parsecmd() " then " compile() " then a "
test-only
.BR codeshape() " dump, the same role " treeshape() " plays for a"
parsed tree - not a real
.I rc
facility, since real debug tracing formats one instruction at a time
as it EXECUTES, and nothing here executes anything yet). See BUGS for
three real bugs this slice's own tests found and fixed, all in this
translation, not upstream.
.B Running
.B rc
does nothing but populate its internal tables and print a note saying
so; there is no shell to use yet.
.SH FILES
.B utils/rcsh/
in this tree - the real source being translated.
.SH SOURCE
.B /p9/cmd/rc.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR mk (1),
.B doc/plan9-terminal-plan.md
in this tree - the sizing and sequencing document this translation
follows.
.SH BUGS
Three real bugs in this translation's own
.BR code.c ,
all found by
.IR rctest (1)'s
own tests failing first, none in the upstream source: the
.B '='
case's real source reuses ONE mutable
.B t
throughout via the
.BR c0 / c1 / c2
macros
.RI ( t->child[0..2] ,
re-evaluated fresh at each use, so their meaning changes every time
.B t
itself is reassigned) and explicitly resets
.B t=tt
right before its own
.BR break ,
"so tests below will work" (its own comment) - a first translation
here dropped that reset, assuming Limbo's own case-arm scoping would
protect the function's outer
.B t
parameter from an inner
.B "t = tt"
assignment; it does not, since that's a plain reassignment of the
SAME shared parameter, not a new shadowing declaration, so the
parameter was left pointing at
.I nil
(exactly where the reused loop variable's own exit condition leaves
it) by the time the epilogue past the whole
.B case
block dereferenced it. Reproducible with nothing more than
.BR "testcompile(\(dqx=5\(dq, 0)" ,
a bare assignment with no trailing command - crashed the whole
.B emu
process outright, not merely a wrong answer. Fixed by restoring the
real source's own
.B t=tt
reset rather than renaming the loop variable to route around the
question of Limbo's scoping rules.
.PP
.BR codeswitch() 's
own translation had two further real bugs of the identical kind
(real code's single mutable
.B t
walked through fresh
.BR c0 / c1
macros, mistranslated by introducing separate-purpose local variables
instead): its own
.B tt
was computed from the constant outer BRACE node
.RI ( t.child[1] ,
never changing) rather than from the CURRENT switch-body position's
own second child, and the loop that compiles a case line's own value
words used two disconnected walks instead of real code's single
.B "for(t=c0->child[0];t->type==ARGLIST;t=c0) outcode(c1,eflag);"
loop - producing a structurally different, wrong instruction sequence
for every switch with more than the most trivial body. Both caught by
comparing this slice's own test expectation - derived from a
from-scratch hand-trace of the real algorithm, done BEFORE running
anything - against what the function actually emitted, then fixing
the function to match the trace once the trace itself was confirmed
correct. See
.BR codeswitch() 's
own header comment in
.B rc.b
for the full derivation.
.PP
No execution engine yet - every slice built so far, now including the
grammar, was chosen specifically because it has real, testable value
semantics without one. The lexer turned out translatable and testable
on its own, despite real
.IR rc 's
own lexer and grammar not being independent of each other there
(yacc generates the token constants the lexer returns) - this
version supplies its own token constants directly (the
.B Rc
module's own
.B Tokbase
declaration), so nothing here actually needs a grammar to exist
first, unlike the real source. The grammar itself turned out
translatable the same recursive-descent way
.IR hoc (1)
translates a yacc-based real source, once the priority table's real
precedence (two lines) was separated from its shift/reduce-conflict
bookkeeping (everything else) - see DESCRIPTION for the full trace.
.PP
Two real findings, both in real
.BR pcmd.c 's
own
.B DUP
case, surfaced by the sixth slice's own round-trip tests
(see DESCRIPTION). First: that case prints
.B only
its own bracket syntax
.RI ( ">[fd0=fd1]" ) " - unlike every other redirection case, it prints"
neither
.I child0
(a
.B DUP
token never has one -
.B "redir: DUP"
takes no operand) nor
.IR child1 ,
so a DUP-prefixed or DUP-percolated command's own rest-of-command
text is silently absent from its
.B own
display string. Real, not a translation gap:
.BR code.c 's
own
.B DUP
case still emits
.B "outcode(c1, eflag)"
regardless of what
.B pcmd()
prints, so once
.B code.c
exists, execution of the rest of the command is unaffected - only
this display text drops it. Second, more surprising: printing a tree
parsed from
.B >[2=1]
genuinely produces
.B >[1=2]
- the two numbers come back swapped. Traced to two independently
real facts, not one translation bug:
.BR lex.c 's
own bracket parser stores the fd
.I before
.B =
into
.B fd1
and the fd
.I after
.B =
into
.B fd0
.RI ( "t->fd1=t->fd0; t->fd0=0;"
before re-parsing the second number - already the exact behavior
slice 4's own lexer tests pin), while
.BR pcmd.c 's
.B DUP
case prints
.B "fd0=fd1"
in that order. Both verified directly against
.BR utils/rcsh/lex.c / pcmd.c ;
this translation reproduces the same round-trip mismatch, it does not
introduce one.
.PP
Two real findings surfaced by the grammar's own tests, both caught by
a test that failed first, not by inspection:
.B !
(BANG) and
.B @
(SUBSHELL) are deliberately absent from
.BR lex.c 's
own
.BR wordchr() " stop-character set (checked directly against"
.BR utils/rcsh/lex.c ),
so with no following space neither is its own token at all -
.B !a
lexes as the single plain word
.BR !a ,
not BANG followed by a word. Real
.I rc
genuinely requires the space; a first draft of this grammar's own
test assumed otherwise and got a very different, but not obviously
wrong-looking, parse tree back. And this parser's own
.B pskipnl()
(the token-level equivalent of real
.BR skipnl() ,
used everywhere a grammar action needs to skip a run of blank/
comment-only lines - after an
.IR if / while / for
condition's closing paren, before its body) exists because the raw,
character-level
.B skipnl()
this file's lexer section already has is
.B wrong
to call from here: by the time a grammar action reaches that point,
this parser has already
.B padvance() 'd
one token of lookahead past the closing
.BR ) ,
unlike real yacc, which for a definite reduce like
.B "paren: '(' body ')'"
does not fetch a lookahead token for the following
.I cmd
until
.B after
the mid-rule action runs. Calling the raw, stream-level
.B skipnl()
here would skip characters out from under an already-fetched token,
leaving it stuck on a stale newline while the underlying stream moved
on past it - found by a multi-line
.B "if(a)\en\etb"
test that silently dropped
.B b
out of the tree entirely.
.PP
A third, smaller finding, from the same tests, derived directly from
.B syn.y
itself rather than checked against any real-world script (no
.I rc
man page was available to consult here): real
.B "SWITCH word"
takes a bare
.IR word ,
not a dedicated condition syntax the way IF/WHILE's own
.I paren
does - there is no such rule in the grammar at all. So
.B switch(x)
written with no space parses as SWITCH followed by
.IR word 's
.B "own generic"
.B "'(' words ')'"
paren-word-list form (the same rule
.B "x=(a b c)"
would also use for a parenthesized word-list, per that same grammar),
giving a PAREN-wrapped one-word list, not a condition - after this
file's own first test draft assumed the C-like reading and
failed.
.PP
A real, non-obvious finding about
.IR rc 's
own semantics, caught by a wrong test expectation rather than assumed:
.B skipwhite()
only consumes leading space/tab/cr and a comment's own body text - the
newline that ends a comment line is
.B not
silently discarded whitespace. It becomes a real
.B '\en'
.B token
(the statement separator real
.IR rc 's
grammar uses), the same as any other newline. A first draft of this
lexer's own test assumed full C-style skipping, expected a word token
directly after a comment, and got the newline token instead - which
then crashed the very next assertion by dereferencing a
.I nil
.B Tree
(correct for a bare single-character token) without checking first.
Both the wrong expectation and the missing nil guard were fixed, not
worked around.
.PP
A second real finding, this time in the
.B upstream C source itself,
not this translation: the check that rejects duplicating a heredoc or
append file descriptor
.RI ( t->type==DUP && (t->rtype==HERE || t->rtype==APPEND) )
is unreachable dead code in
.BR utils/rcsh/lex.c .
Traced by hand against the real source after a test expecting
.B <<[2=1]
to fail instead succeeded:
.B t->type
only ever becomes
.B DUP
inside the
.B if(c=='=')
block, and every path through that block unconditionally overwrites
.B t->rtype
to
.B DUPFD
or
.B CLOSE
first - so the HERE/APPEND check can never see what it's looking for.
Real
.I rc
genuinely accepts
.B <<[2=1]
and
.B >>[2=1]
without complaint. This translation reproduces that faithfully, not
the naive reading of what the check appears to intend.
.PP
Real
.IR rc 's
own
.B Treenodes
/
.B freenodes
mechanism - a bulk-allocation list kept only so every parse-tree node
can be freed at once - has no
.B next
field counterpart in this version's
.B Tree
at all, not just an omitted free path like the rest of this list: under
a real garbage collector the whole bookkeeping list has nothing to do.
.PP
.B match() 's
real source manually decodes UTF-8 byte sequences itself, because C's
.B char*
has no idea what a multi-byte code point is. Limbo's
.B string
type already is a rune sequence, so this translation indexes strings
directly instead - a real simplification, not a mechanical translation,
noted in
.BR rc.b 's
own header comment.
.PP
.BR match() 's
.B [...]
bracket-class case has one deliberate behavioral divergence from the
real source: a pattern that runs out before its closing
.B ]
returns 0 (no match) here, where the real source would read past its
own string's NUL terminator - undefined behavior in C, but a Limbo
array-bounds crash if translated as-is, since Limbo strings check
every index at runtime. Only a malformed pattern is affected; a
well-formed one behaves identically.
.PP
Real
.I rc
frees
.BR Word / List / Var / Redir
cells explicitly throughout the source translated so far. Limbo has a
real garbage collector; every one of those free paths is simply
omitted, not translated - stated once here rather than at each
omission.
.PP
.B vlook
in the real source consults the currently running thread's local
variables before falling back to the global table. There is no
Thread yet, so this version's
.B vlook
always falls back to the global table - correct once there is
exactly one, implicit scope (true right now, since nothing here can
create a local variable yet), and due to change once
.B code.c
and
.B exec.c
introduce a real running thread with real local scopes.
